On 2008-05-05, bohemian <glen.edmonds@gmail.com> wrote:
> A "scroll of rescue".  It's like a amulet of LS, except if you die as
> well as saving your life, you get rescued: ie teleported  - across
> levels if necessary - to the spot where you read the scroll.  That way
> you don't go "back in time" to retry things if you didn't like the
> result; it's just the next turn.  The benefit is that you don't take
> up a slot.  The drawback is that you have to go right back and do it
> all again.

There's no drawback to this; in fact, it's a significant advantage over
the amulet of lifesaving in that:

-- It doesn't take up my neck slot
-- It doesn't leave me right in the middle of whatever killed me
-- It can be written with a magic marker

Seriously, the AoLS is good enough.  There's no need to keep bolting on
new and improved ideas to avoid death; once you're into the midgame,
it's just not that hard to refrain from dying.

(There is also the consideration to be taken of how this interacts with
Astral and the Planes when read on DL1 -- the game currently deletes all
those levels when you ascend the stairs.  While it's a simple enough
solution to say "It quits working", the Planes are actually one of the
few places post-midgame that a player actually is more likely to die;
defeating the purpose and confusing the player.)

